.\" Manual page for FileManager, the MD5 duplicate-file finder in SystemUtils.
.\"
.\" Read it without installing:   man -l man/filemanager.1
.\" Check it for troff errors:    groff -man -Tutf8 -ww -z man/filemanager.1
.\" Regenerate the markdown copy:  scripts/render_man.py
.\"
.\" THIS is the source. man/filemanager.md is generated from it and is
.\" compared against a fresh render by tests/test_man_page.py, so edit here.
.\"
.\" Every exit code, default and output excerpt here was taken from a run of
.\" src/main.py, not from the source comments. When the CLI changes, change
.\" this page in the same commit -- a manual page that disagrees with the
.\" program is worse than no manual page, because it is believed.
.TH FILEMANAGER 1 2026-11-19 SystemUtils "FileManager Manual"
.
.SH NAME
filemanager \- index files by MD5 checksum and report duplicates across filesystems
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY filemanager
.OP \-\-version
.OP \-\-help
.YS
.SY filemanager
.I action
.RI [ path ]
.OP \-\-db\-path path
.OP \-\-threads n
.OP \-\-prefix prefix
.OP \-\-exclude\-prefix list
.OP \-\-min\-duplicates n
.OP \-\-dirA path
.OP \-\-dirB path
.OP \-\-regex
.OP \-\-dry\-run
.OP \-\-prune\-missing\-dirs
.YS
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B filemanager
walks one or more directory trees, computes an MD5 checksum for every regular
file it finds, and records
.RI ( md5sum ", " size ", " last_modified ", " path )
in a SQLite database.
The database is then queried to find files whose contents are identical,
including across separate filesystems and mount points, which is what
duplicate finders scoped to a single tree cannot do.
.PP
Its output is a list of files a human may then delete.
A wrong answer therefore has consequences past this process, and the program is
built around that: a question it cannot answer is reported as unanswered rather
than guessed, and an action that completed only partly says so in its exit
status.
.PP
.B Only regular files are read or recorded, and a symlink is never followed.
Every other entry \(em a symlink, whatever it points at; a character or block
device; a FIFO; a socket \(em is skipped without being opened, logged as
.RB ` "NOT REGULAR (\fIkind\fP), skipped: \fIpath\fP" ',
and counted by kind for the run's summary.
A skip is not a failure, because nothing that belongs in the index was missed.
A copy of
.I /dev/random
never reaches end of file and a FIFO blocks the
.BR open (2)
until a writer appears, so reading either one made a scan never finish.
A root backup made with
.RB ` "rsync \-a" '
recreates both, so any backup volume can hold them (#74).
.
.SS The skip check
Rescanning a large tree is cheap because
.B scan
and
.B audit\-db
read the stored
.I size
and
.I last_modified
for each path and return without hashing when both still match.
.I last_modified
is stored as integer milliseconds,
.RB ` int(os.path.getmtime(path)\ *\ 1000) '.
.PP
The consequence matters when choosing an action:
.B the skip check is blind to any change that does not alter size or mtime.
Bit rot, a bad sector, a failing cable and a silently truncated copy all rewrite
bytes without touching either, so those are exactly the files
.B scan
skips.
.BR verify\-db ,
and only
.BR verify\-db ,
rehashes unconditionally.
.
.SS Invocation
There are two supported forms, and they differ in which interpreter runs.
.TP
.B From a checkout
.RB ` "python src/main.py" '
run from the
.I FileManager
directory.
.B src/main.py
prepends its parent to
.I sys.path
and every module imports its siblings as
.IR src.<module> ,
so running it from any other working directory breaks the imports.
.TP
.B From the bundle
.RB ` "./FileManager.pyz" ',
a single-file Python
.B zipapp
archive built by
.IR scripts/build\-pyz.sh .
It pins
.RB ` "/usr/bin/python3 \-I" '
in its shebang and depends on nothing outside the standard library, which is
what makes it safe to run as another user.
.PP
.B Do not run
.RB ` "sudo python src/main.py" '.
.BR sudo (8)
resolves
.B python
from root's
.IR PATH ,
not from the invoking user's
.BR pyenv (1)
shim, so it silently selects a different interpreter or none at all.
Use the bundle for privileged runs.
The name under which the bundle is installed is the installer's choice; this
page calls it
.BR filemanager .
.
.SH ACTIONS
Exactly one action is required, as the first non-option argument.
.BR scan ", " check\-file ", " scan\-dir\-report ", " remove\-record " and " get\-file\-info
also require
.IR path .
.
.TP
.BI scan " path\fR[\fP,path...\fR]\fP"
Walk each directory or file in the comma-separated list and record a checksum
for every regular file, honouring the skip check.
All paths share one tally, because the caller asked for one scan and so gets one
answer about whether it was complete.
Prints
.RB ` "scan complete: N hashed, N skipped unchanged, N failed" '
and then every failed path.
When anything was skipped because it is not a regular file, a second line
follows:
.RB ` "skipped N not regular file(s), not read: fifo N, symlink N" ',
with one count per kind.
.B Exits 3 if anything failed;
a skipped entry does not count.
A listed path that is neither a regular file nor a directory counts as a
failure, and a symlink named on the command line is not followed.
.TP
.BI check\-file " path"
Hash one file and print any recorded paths holding the same checksum.
Does not record anything.
Exits 1 if
.I path
is not a regular file.
.TP
.BI scan\-dir\-report " path"
Scan one directory and report the duplicates found within it in the same run.
.TP
.B report\-duplicates
Print every checksum in the database held by at least
.B \-\-min\-duplicates
files, with the paths under each.
With
.BR \-\-prefix ,
report duplicates only under that prefix \(em and note that
.B \-\-min\-duplicates
is then not applied, because the two are separate queries.
.TP
.B audit\-db
Re-verify every recorded path: drop rows for files that no longer exist, rehash
those whose
.RI ( size ", " mtime )
changed.
.B This action deletes rows; run it with \-\-dry\-run first.
Rows whose parent directory is also missing are reported as
.B SUSPECT
and
.B not
removed, because that is what an unmounted volume looks like \(em
.B \-\-prune\-missing\-dirs
removes them deliberately.
A row whose path is no longer a regular file \(em a symlink recorded by a
version before #74, for example \(em is removed too, under the same
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
and counted as
.BR "not regular" .
Only a path that does not exist counts as missing.
A path that cannot be examined, such as one under a directory the auditor
cannot search, counts as a failure and its row is kept.
Prints its settings and their origins before doing anything, then
.RB ` "audit complete: ..." '.
.B Exits 3 if anything failed.
This is the only action that sets
.RB ` "PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL" ',
so a database that has never been audited is still in rollback-journal mode.
.TP
.B verify\-db
Rehash recorded files and compare each against the
.B stored
checksum, reporting every mismatch with both values.
.B The only action that can detect silent corruption,
for the reason given under
.B The skip check
above.
.B Never writes to the database:
a verify that repaired the baseline on a mismatch would destroy the only
evidence of corruption and would pass on its second run.
Three outcomes, never two \(em matched, mismatched, could not read \(em and an
unreadable file is
.B not
a pass.
A recorded path that is not a regular file is not opened, and is reported as
unreadable;
.B audit\-db
removes such rows.
Scope a long run with
.BR \-\-prefix ;
verifying millions of recorded files takes hours.
.B Exits 4 on a mismatch, 3 on an unreadable file,
and 4 wins when both occur.
.TP
.B report\-duplicate\-sizes
Print the total size occupied by duplicate files.
.TP
.BI report\-prefix\-count " \fB\-\-prefix\fP prefix"
Print how many recorded paths begin with
.IR prefix .
Requires
.BR \-\-prefix .
.TP
.BI report\-files\-for\-prefix " \fB\-\-prefix\fP prefix"
Print every recorded path beginning with
.IR prefix .
Requires
.BR \-\-prefix .
Accepted by the parser but absent from the program's own
.B help
output; see
.B BUGS
below.
.TP
.BI remove\-record " path"
Remove the record for
.B exactly
.IR path .
With
.BR \-\-regex ,
.I path
is instead a regular expression matched against the
.B whole
recorded path
.RB ( re.fullmatch ).
Removing a subtree is therefore explicit:
.RB ` \(aq/a/b/.*\(aq '.
Prints the number of rows removed.
.TP
.BI compare\-directories " \fB\-\-dirA\fP a \fB\-\-dirB\fP b"
Hash both trees in full, in process, and report the files unique to each.
.B Ignores the database entirely
\(em it is an O(n\(mum) comparison of two freshly hashed trees, not a query.
Requires both
.B \-\-dirA
and
.BR \-\-dirB .
.TP
.BI get\-file\-info " path"
Print the size, stored modification time and checksum the database holds for one
path.
.TP
.BI scan\-unique\-files " path"
Scan a directory and report the files whose checksum appears nowhere else.
.B Writes into the directory it scans:
a
.I .processed_files.txt
resume log at the root of the target tree, read back on the next run.
Delete it to force a full recheck.
Because the log lands inside the scanned tree, the next walk finds and checks it
like any other file, so a second run over an unchanged tree reports exactly one
file: the log.
A file whose uniqueness could not be determined is logged as
.B unreadable
rather than reported either way, and is
.B rechecked
on the next run; a verdict is final, "could not check" is not.
.TP
.B help
Print the program's own usage text and exit 0.
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help " and " \-?
do the same.
Note that
.B ?
is a shell glob character and needs quoting as
.RB ` \(aq\-?\(aq '.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the commit this code was built from, the build time and whether the source
tree was clean, then exit 0.
A bundle reports the values embedded when it was built; a source checkout says
so and reads
.BR git (1)
live.
A question git cannot answer prints
.BR "CANNOT DETERMINE" ,
never a placeholder and never
.BR clean .
The clean/dirty check is scoped to the
.I FileManager
directory only, so an edit elsewhere in the repository does not make a bundle's
source dirty.
Takes precedence over every action;
.B \-\-help
in turn takes precedence over
.BR \-\-version ,
since a caller asking both questions at once is asking how to use the tool.
.TP
.BI \-\-db\-path " path"
The database file, or a directory to hold one.
If
.I path
is an existing directory,
.I file_manager.db
is appended to it.
.B Default:
.I ./file_manager.db
in the current working directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " n"
Width of the hashing pool.
.BR "Default: 4" .
Threads are the only concurrency model; no database connection is ever shared
between them.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " prefix"
Required by
.B report\-prefix\-count
and
.BR report\-files\-for\-prefix .
For
.B verify\-db
it scopes the run to recorded paths under
.IR prefix ,
which is how a verify is made to fit in an available window.
For
.B report\-duplicates
it switches the query to prefix mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-prefix " list"
.B audit\-db
only: comma-separated prefixes whose paths are left untouched by the audit.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-duplicates " n"
.B report\-duplicates
only: report a checksum only when at least
.I n
files hold it.
.BR "Default: 2" .
.B It counts copies, not copies beyond the first
\(em 2 means a file and at least one other like it, so
.B \-\-min\-duplicates 3
returns groups of three.
.TP
.BI \-\-dirA " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-dirB " path"
The two trees
.B compare\-directories
hashes.
Both are required by that action and ignored by every other.
.TP
.B \-\-regex
.B remove\-record
only: treat
.I path
as a regular expression matched against the whole recorded path.
Without it the argument is taken literally.
The regex form is opt-in because this action's argument looks like a path, and a
path silently interpreted as a pattern deletes siblings.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
.B audit\-db
only: report every change the audit would make, and make none of them.
.TP
.B \-\-prune\-missing\-dirs
.B audit\-db
only: also remove rows whose parent directory is gone.
Off by default; see
.B audit\-db
above for why.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
An invalid invocation exits non-zero, so a caller under
.RB ` "set \-e" '
stops.
.TP
.B 0
The action completed and read everything it was asked to read, or help or
.B \-\-version
was requested.
.TP
.B 1
A required argument was missing or invalid.
Nothing was recorded.
.TP
.B 2
The argument parser rejected the command line \(em an unknown option, or an action outside the
accepted set.
Nothing was recorded.
.TP
.B 3
.BR scan ", " audit\-db " or " verify\-db
completed but could not read part of what it was asked to read.
.B Part of the tree was recorded or checked and part was not.
The counts and every failed path are on standard output.
.IP
3 is deliberately neither 1 nor 2: those mean nothing happened at all, while
this leaves a partial index behind \(em and a partial index
.B cannot be detected afterwards,
because the database looks the same whether a file was absent from the tree or
merely unreadable.
.B This is the status an unattended run must check.
.TP
.B 4
.B verify\-db
found a recorded file whose contents no longer match its stored checksum.
Not 3: 3 means part of the answer is unknown, 4 means the answer is known and
bad, and an operator does something different about each.
A mismatch outranks an unreadable file when both occur in one run.
.
.SH OUTPUT
.B The split between the two streams is a contract, not a convention.
A scheduled scan is normally run with
.RB ` 2>/dev/null '
to suppress the per-file log, so anything that is the
.B result
of the run goes to standard output and survives that redirection.
.TP
.B Standard output
The run header; the per-action report; the scan, audit and verify summaries with
their failed and mismatched paths; and, during a scan, a bare
.B .
per skipped file.
Each summary is preceded by a blank line, because those dots otherwise leave it
at the end of a very long one.
Failure lists are truncated at 20 entries, with a count of the remainder; every
one of them is still on its own log line.
.TP
.B Standard error
All logging, at
.BR INFO ,
in the form
.RB ` "%(asctime)s \- %(levelname)s \- %(message)s" '.
.PP
Every action that runs prints a header first, before it creates or opens
anything:
.PP
.EX
FileManager run
started : 2026\-11\-17 17:53:54 MST
version : 1c720f402037ce3f4f6941b2bb7d893fcf10bb93 (source checkout, clean)
action  : scan
paths   : /home2
db      : /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db
threads : 2
.EE
.PP
The zone is part of the requirement, not decoration: this output is read out of
a log days later, and a bare clock time gets read against the wrong zone.
.B help
and
.B \-\-version
print no header \(em they are questions about the tool rather than runs of it.
.PP
.B audit\-db
and
.B verify\-db
additionally print each setting with its
.B origin
before doing any work:
.PP
.EX
verify\-db settings:
  db_path = /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db  (set)
  threads = 4  (default)
  prefix  = (every recorded path)  (default)
.EE
.PP
.RB ` (set) '
versus
.RB ` (default) '
is the point.
A value that merely looks right is not evidence that it was set, and
.B audit\-db
deletes rows \(em the configuration that governed a past run is not recoverable
from its effects.
A setting whose name looks like a credential is refused outright rather than
printed, because this output is written to disk.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./file_manager.db
The database, when
.B \-\-db\-path
is not given.
.TP
.IR <scanned\ tree> /.processed_files.txt
.B scan\-unique\-files
resume log, written inside the directory being scanned.
.TP
.I dist/FileManager.pyz
The bundle, built by
.IR scripts/build\-pyz.sh .
.PP
.B Two notes on the database file.
Its parent directory is
.B not
created for you: name a directory that does not exist and the run fails with an
uncaught SQLite error rather than a message (see
.BR BUGS ).
And every action except
.B help
and
.B \-\-version
opens and initialises the database before dispatching, so even
.BR compare\-directories ,
which never queries it, creates an empty one as a side effect.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B The program itself reads no environment variables.
The following are read by the tooling around it and are listed because they are
easily mistaken for program options.
.TP
.B FM_ALLOW_DIRTY
Read by
.IR scripts/build\-pyz.sh .
The build refuses to run while anything under
.I FileManager/
is uncommitted, because the artifact would then be unidentifiable.
Setting it to exactly
.B 1
builds anyway, and the artifact reports
.B "dirty at build time"
permanently.
.TP
.BR FM_DB ", " FM_THREADS ", " FM_VIDEO_DIRS
Read by the
.I fm\-*.sh
wrapper scripts to override the database, the thread count and the scanned
directories.
.TP
.B VIRTUAL_ENV
Not read deliberately, but worth knowing about: a shell that inherited it from
another project silently beats the
.BR pyenv (1)
shim, so a bare
.B python
can be a different interpreter from the one
.RB ` "pyenv version" '
reports.
The wrappers use
.RB ` "pyenv exec" '
for exactly this reason.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Index two trees with eight threads, keeping the database off both of them:
.PP
.EX
filemanager \-\-threads 8 \-\-db\-path /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db \e
    scan /home2,/mnt/internal/BACKUP/00
.EE
.PP
Run the same scan from
.BR cron (8),
checking the status that matters:
.PP
.EX
filemanager \-\-threads 8 \-\-db\-path /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db \e
    scan /home2 2>/dev/null
case $? in
    0) ;;                                  # complete
    3) echo "PARTIAL INDEX \- see the failed paths above" >&2 ;;
    *) echo "scan did not run" >&2 ;;
esac
.EE
.PP
Report what the audit would delete, before letting it delete anything:
.PP
.EX
filemanager \-\-db\-path /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db audit\-db \-\-dry\-run
filemanager \-\-db\-path /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db audit\-db \e
    \-\-exclude\-prefix /mnt/offline,/mnt/other
.EE
.PP
Read the stored checksums back for one volume, which is the only way to find
corruption that has already happened:
.PP
.EX
filemanager \-\-threads 8 \-\-db\-path /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db \e
    verify\-db \-\-prefix /mnt/internal/BACKUP/00
.EE
.PP
Report groups of three or more identical files, then remove a subtree's records:
.PP
.EX
filemanager \-\-db\-path /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db \e
    report\-duplicates \-\-min\-duplicates 3
filemanager \-\-db\-path /var/lib/filemanager/home2.db \e
    remove\-record \(aq/mnt/retired/.*\(aq \-\-regex
.EE
.PP
Ask an installed bundle which commit it is:
.PP
.EX
filemanager \-\-version
FileManager
commit : 1c720f402037ce3f4f6941b2bb7d893fcf10bb93
built  : 2026\-11\-17 13:50:31 MST
tree   : clean
source : zipapp bundle
.EE
.
.SH DATABASE
One table, created by every run that reaches an action:
.PP
.EX
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS files (
    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
    md5sum        TEXT,
    size          INTEGER,
    last_modified INTEGER,
    path          TEXT UNIQUE
)
.EE
.PP
There are no indexes beyond the implicit unique index on
.IR path ,
so duplicate reporting is a full table scan and group-by.
.PP
.B Any change to how last_modified is stored or compared invalidates every
.B existing database
and forces a full rehash of every recorded file, because the skip check compares
the stored value directly.
.PP
Connections are never shared or reused: one is opened per unit of work, used,
and closed.
A locked database is retried up to 10 times at 0.1s intervals, catching
.B only
an
.B OperationalError
containing "database is locked" and re-raising anything else, so a malformed
statement fails immediately rather than a second later.
.
.SH NOTES
.SS Choosing between scan and verify\-db
They answer different questions and neither substitutes for the other.
.B scan
answers "is the index current?" cheaply, by trusting
.RI ( size ", " mtime ).
.B verify\-db
answers "is what the index says still true?" expensively, by rehashing.
.PP
Whether that second question needs asking depends on the filesystem.
On one with data checksums \(em
.BR btrfs (5),
ZFS \(em the kernel already detects corruption per block, and
.BR btrfs\-scrub (8)
is the cheaper tool for it.
On
.BR ext4 (5),
which checksums metadata only, an application-level verify is the only thing
that will notice a file whose contents changed without its mtime.
.
.SS Memory
.B scan
holds at most 68,759 queued file paths in memory, about 8\ MiB for paths of
67 characters; the walk waits when the hashing workers fall behind (#67).
Longer paths cost about one byte more per character.
.B verify\-db
is not bounded: it materialises every row it will check before it starts, on the order of
1.3\ GiB for 5.6\ million rows; see
.BR BUGS .
.
.SS Database placement
Keep the database off the trees being scanned, and on an SSD if there is one.
A scan opens a connection per file and commits one transaction per recorded
file, so the database is the write-heavy part of the run; putting it inside a
scanned tree additionally makes it an input to the next walk.
.
.SH BUGS
Known and open, with the issue numbers in
.UR https://github.com/users/JeffreyPeacock/projects/14
the SystemUtils board
.UE :
.TP
.B verify\-db materialises every row (#68)
The recorded paths are read into a list before any hashing starts, rather than
streamed.
Use
.B \-\-prefix
to bound it.
.TP
.B A missing database directory is reported as an invalid argument (#69)
Naming a
.B \-\-db\-path
whose parent directory does not exist produces an uncaught
.B sqlite3.OperationalError
and a traceback, exiting 1 \(em a status this page defines as a bad argument.
Create the directory first.
.TP
.B report\-files\-for\-prefix is undocumented and validated late
The action is accepted by the parser but does not appear in the program's own
.B help
output, and its
.B \-\-prefix
check runs after the header has been printed and the database created, rather
than alongside the other argument checks.
.
